1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,775
Take an empty plastic bottle down to

2
00:00:02,775 --> 00:00:05,474
the bottom of a deep swimming pool,

3
00:00:05,474 --> 00:00:07,093
and it gets squeezed.

4
00:00:07,273 --> 00:00:10,525
Now imagine going three hundred metres under

5
00:00:10,525 --> 00:00:11,116
the sea.

6
00:00:11,296 --> 00:00:14,080
The water pushes on every square metre

7
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:16,498
of a submarine with the weight of

8
00:00:16,498 --> 00:00:17,891
hundreds of tonnes.

9
00:00:18,071 --> 00:00:19,483
And yet, inside,

10
00:00:19,483 --> 00:00:22,662
the crew walks around in normal air,

11
00:00:22,662 --> 00:00:24,340
at normal pressure.

12
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:27,638
Today, ten parts of a submarine,

13
00:00:27,638 --> 00:00:29,977
and why each one exists.

14
00:00:30,326 --> 00:00:32,821
Every ten metres of sea water adds

15
00:00:32,821 --> 00:00:35,609
about one more atmosphere of pressure.

16
00:00:35,789 --> 00:00:37,440
At three hundred metres,

17
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:40,330
the water pushes about thirty times harder

18
00:00:40,330 --> 00:00:42,256
than the air at the surface.

19
00:00:42,436 --> 00:00:45,080
A submarine is a machine built to

20
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:46,442
resist that push,

21
00:00:46,442 --> 00:00:48,045
while staying quiet,

22
00:00:48,045 --> 00:00:50,609
hidden and able to come back up.

23
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:54,107
As always, every fact here comes from

24
00:00:54,107 --> 00:00:55,383
a named source,

25
00:00:55,383 --> 00:00:57,765
navies and naval references,

26
00:00:57,765 --> 00:01:00,317
all listed in the description.

27
00:01:00,497 --> 00:01:03,599
Some details, like exact diving depths,

28
00:01:03,599 --> 00:01:06,145
are secret, so we will stay with

29
00:01:06,145 --> 00:01:07,974
what is publicly known.

30
00:01:08,154 --> 00:01:09,443
Let's start with the hull.

31
00:01:09,793 --> 00:01:12,384
Why is a submarine shaped like a

32
00:01:12,384 --> 00:01:13,194
long tube?

33
00:01:13,374 --> 00:01:15,801
Because a cylinder is one of the

34
00:01:15,801 --> 00:01:18,759
best shapes to resist pressure from all

35
00:01:18,759 --> 00:01:19,214
sides.

36
00:01:19,394 --> 00:01:21,013
Inside the outer skin,

37
00:01:21,013 --> 00:01:23,514
there is a thick steel tube called

38
00:01:23,514 --> 00:01:24,838
the pressure hull,

39
00:01:24,838 --> 00:01:26,383
where the crew lives.

40
00:01:26,563 --> 00:01:29,461
The water presses evenly all around the

41
00:01:29,461 --> 00:01:32,656
circle, and the steel works in compression,

42
00:01:32,656 --> 00:01:34,588
the way an arch holds up a

43
00:01:34,588 --> 00:01:35,108
bridge.

44
00:01:35,459 --> 00:01:38,503
The steel is a special high strength

45
00:01:38,503 --> 00:01:41,716
alloy, and the tube is reinforced from

46
00:01:41,716 --> 00:01:44,760
the inside with rings called frames,

47
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:46,790
spaced along its length.

48
00:01:46,970 --> 00:01:49,537
A flat surface would bend under the

49
00:01:49,537 --> 00:01:50,197
pressure.

50
00:01:50,377 --> 00:01:53,226
A curved one spreads the force around

51
00:01:53,226 --> 00:01:53,918
the ring.

52
00:01:54,098 --> 00:01:56,848
Even the ends of the pressure hull

53
00:01:56,848 --> 00:01:58,626
are domed, never flat.

54
00:01:58,977 --> 00:02:01,567
Even so, the hull is squeezed a

55
00:02:01,567 --> 00:02:04,073
little as the submarine dives.

56
00:02:04,253 --> 00:02:07,117
Sailors have told stories of a string

57
00:02:07,117 --> 00:02:10,600
stretched across a compartment going slack at

58
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:13,773
depth, as the hull gets slightly smaller.

59
00:02:13,953 --> 00:02:15,313
The steel bends,

60
00:02:15,313 --> 00:02:17,184
but it does not break,

61
00:02:17,184 --> 00:02:20,244
as long as the submarine stays above

62
00:02:20,244 --> 00:02:21,094
its limit.

63
00:02:21,443 --> 00:02:24,108
Why does every submarine have a strict

64
00:02:24,108 --> 00:02:24,950
depth limit?

65
00:02:25,130 --> 00:02:26,977
Because there is a depth where the

66
00:02:26,977 --> 00:02:27,846
hull would fail.

67
00:02:28,026 --> 00:02:30,238
Navies define a test depth,

68
00:02:30,238 --> 00:02:33,515
the deepest the submarine is designed to

69
00:02:33,515 --> 00:02:35,481
go in normal operations,

70
00:02:35,481 --> 00:02:37,939
and a much deeper crush depth,

71
00:02:37,939 --> 00:02:41,134
where the hull is expected to collapse.

72
00:02:41,314 --> 00:02:43,760
The gap between them is the safety

73
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:44,263
margin.

74
00:02:44,612 --> 00:02:47,661
The exact numbers are secret for most

75
00:02:47,661 --> 00:02:52,028
military submarines, but modern attack submarines are

76
00:02:52,028 --> 00:02:55,982
generally believed to operate at several hundred

77
00:02:55,982 --> 00:02:56,559
metres.

78
00:02:56,739 --> 00:02:58,884
Going deeper means a thicker,

79
00:02:58,884 --> 00:03:01,546
heavier hull, or stronger materials.

80
00:03:01,726 --> 00:03:04,404
Every metre of depth is a trade

81
00:03:04,404 --> 00:03:06,822
with weight, speed and cost.

82
00:03:07,172 --> 00:03:10,568
The Soviet Union built some submarines with

83
00:03:10,568 --> 00:03:13,964
titanium hulls, which are strong and light.

84
00:03:14,144 --> 00:03:15,116
One of them,

85
00:03:15,116 --> 00:03:18,438
the Komsomolets, reached a depth of about

86
00:03:18,438 --> 00:03:22,002
one thousand metres in nineteen eighty-four,

87
00:03:22,002 --> 00:03:24,756
a record for a military submarine.

88
00:03:24,936 --> 00:03:29,190
Titanium was extremely expensive and hard to

89
00:03:29,190 --> 00:03:31,995
weld, so very few were built.

90
00:03:32,344 --> 00:03:35,236
Why does a submarine blast out sprays

91
00:03:35,236 --> 00:03:37,267
of water when it surfaces?

92
00:03:37,447 --> 00:03:39,734
Because of its ballast tanks.

93
00:03:39,914 --> 00:03:43,093
A submarine dives by letting sea water

94
00:03:43,093 --> 00:03:44,515
into large tanks,

95
00:03:44,515 --> 00:03:46,439
which makes it heavier.

96
00:03:46,619 --> 00:03:47,935
To come back up,

97
00:03:47,935 --> 00:03:50,732
it uses compressed air to push the

98
00:03:50,732 --> 00:03:54,022
water out, making the submarine lighter,

99
00:03:54,022 --> 00:03:55,338
so it floats up.

100
00:03:55,688 --> 00:03:58,804
The main ballast tanks sit outside the

101
00:03:58,804 --> 00:04:02,001
pressure hull, between it and the outer

102
00:04:02,001 --> 00:04:02,411
skin.

103
00:04:02,591 --> 00:04:04,401
They do not need to resist the

104
00:04:04,401 --> 00:04:06,755
full pressure, because they are open to

105
00:04:06,755 --> 00:04:08,082
the sea at the bottom.

106
00:04:08,262 --> 00:04:10,525
Air pushed in at the top simply

107
00:04:10,525 --> 00:04:13,153
forces the water out of the openings

108
00:04:13,153 --> 00:04:13,591
below.

109
00:04:13,941 --> 00:04:15,137
In an emergency,

110
00:04:15,137 --> 00:04:17,453
the crew can blow all the tanks

111
00:04:17,453 --> 00:04:19,770
at once with high pressure air.

112
00:04:19,950 --> 00:04:22,851
The submarine rises fast and can burst

113
00:04:22,851 --> 00:04:24,913
out of the water at a steep

114
00:04:24,913 --> 00:04:27,357
angle, throwing up a huge spray.

115
00:04:27,537 --> 00:04:29,976
It is the most dramatic moment in

116
00:04:29,976 --> 00:04:31,380
a submarine's life,

117
00:04:31,380 --> 00:04:33,671
and the last line of defence if

118
00:04:33,671 --> 00:04:35,223
something goes wrong.

119
00:04:35,573 --> 00:04:38,652
Why does a submarine pump water around

120
00:04:38,652 --> 00:04:39,787
inside itself?

121
00:04:39,967 --> 00:04:42,359
To stay perfectly balanced.

122
00:04:42,539 --> 00:04:45,934
Underwater, a submarine should be neither heavier

123
00:04:45,934 --> 00:04:48,497
nor lighter than the water around it,

124
00:04:48,497 --> 00:04:51,200
so it hovers without sinking or rising.

125
00:04:51,380 --> 00:04:53,667
That is called neutral buoyancy.

126
00:04:53,847 --> 00:04:56,609
Small tanks called trim tanks fine tune

127
00:04:56,609 --> 00:04:57,388
the weight.

128
00:04:57,738 --> 00:05:00,315
The weight of a submarine keeps changing.

129
00:05:00,495 --> 00:05:01,843
Food is eaten,

130
00:05:01,843 --> 00:05:03,095
fuel is used,

131
00:05:03,095 --> 00:05:05,022
torpedoes are fired,

132
00:05:05,022 --> 00:05:09,452
and water density changes with temperature and

133
00:05:09,452 --> 00:05:09,934
salt.

134
00:05:10,114 --> 00:05:12,532
Even the crew walking from one end

135
00:05:12,532 --> 00:05:14,665
to the other can tip the boat.

136
00:05:14,845 --> 00:05:17,496
The trim tanks move water forward or

137
00:05:17,496 --> 00:05:20,074
backward, and take water in or pump

138
00:05:20,074 --> 00:05:22,136
it out, to keep the balance.

139
00:05:22,485 --> 00:05:26,015
A well trimmed submarine can hover silently,

140
00:05:26,015 --> 00:05:28,662
with almost no use of its engine.

141
00:05:28,842 --> 00:05:30,661
That is a huge advantage,

142
00:05:30,661 --> 00:05:33,498
because a moving propeller makes noise.

143
00:05:33,678 --> 00:05:36,694
Balancing a ship of thousands of tonnes

144
00:05:36,694 --> 00:05:39,478
to within a few hundred kilograms is

145
00:05:39,478 --> 00:05:41,643
one of the quiet skills of a

146
00:05:41,643 --> 00:05:42,803
submarine crew.

147
00:05:43,152 --> 00:05:46,710
Why are many submarines covered in black

148
00:05:46,710 --> 00:05:47,866
rubber tiles?

149
00:05:48,046 --> 00:05:49,866
They are anechoic tiles,

150
00:05:49,866 --> 00:05:51,761
which means without echo.

151
00:05:51,941 --> 00:05:54,241
An enemy can find a submarine by

152
00:05:54,241 --> 00:05:57,043
sending a sound pulse through the water

153
00:05:57,043 --> 00:05:58,983
and listening for the echo,

154
00:05:58,983 --> 00:06:01,067
which is called active sonar.

155
00:06:01,247 --> 00:06:04,317
The tiles absorb part of that sound,

156
00:06:04,317 --> 00:06:06,193
so the echo is weaker.

157
00:06:06,542 --> 00:06:09,782
The tiles also work from the inside.

158
00:06:09,962 --> 00:06:11,941
Machines on the submarine,

159
00:06:11,941 --> 00:06:13,920
like pumps and generators,

160
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:17,269
make vibrations that travel through the hull

161
00:06:17,269 --> 00:06:18,716
and into the water.

162
00:06:18,896 --> 00:06:21,966
The rubber layer dampens those sounds before

163
00:06:21,966 --> 00:06:24,967
they escape, making the submarine harder to

164
00:06:24,967 --> 00:06:25,316
hear.

165
00:06:25,496 --> 00:06:27,784
The Soviet Union was one of the

166
00:06:27,784 --> 00:06:29,629
first to use them widely.

167
00:06:29,979 --> 00:06:32,924
Look closely at photos of submarines in

168
00:06:32,924 --> 00:06:35,945
port, and you will sometimes see missing

169
00:06:35,945 --> 00:06:38,059
tiles, like gaps in a mouth.

170
00:06:38,239 --> 00:06:40,527
Keeping the tiles glued on is a

171
00:06:40,527 --> 00:06:42,372
constant maintenance job.

172
00:06:42,552 --> 00:06:43,818
For a submarine,

173
00:06:43,818 --> 00:06:46,667
being heard is the biggest danger of

174
00:06:46,667 --> 00:06:49,437
all, more than the pressure itself.

175
00:06:49,786 --> 00:06:52,845
Why do submarines have small wings on

176
00:06:52,845 --> 00:06:54,581
the tower or the bow?

177
00:06:54,761 --> 00:06:56,285
They are diving planes,

178
00:06:56,285 --> 00:06:58,603
and they work like the elevators on

179
00:06:58,603 --> 00:06:59,928
the tail of a plane.

180
00:07:00,108 --> 00:07:02,525
When the submarine is moving,

181
00:07:02,525 --> 00:07:05,525
tilting the planes pushes the bow up

182
00:07:05,525 --> 00:07:08,109
or down, so the boat can change

183
00:07:08,109 --> 00:07:11,776
depth smoothly without changing its ballast.

184
00:07:12,125 --> 00:07:14,214
On many American submarines,

185
00:07:14,214 --> 00:07:16,900
the planes were placed on the tower,

186
00:07:16,900 --> 00:07:18,093
called the sail.

187
00:07:18,273 --> 00:07:20,594
That keeps them away from the noisy

188
00:07:20,594 --> 00:07:21,257
bow sonar.

189
00:07:21,437 --> 00:07:23,662
But planes on the sail must turn

190
00:07:23,662 --> 00:07:26,305
vertical to break through ice when the

191
00:07:26,305 --> 00:07:28,600
submarine surfaces in the Arctic.

192
00:07:28,780 --> 00:07:31,256
Newer designs often move them to the

193
00:07:31,256 --> 00:07:33,320
bow, where they can fold away.

194
00:07:33,669 --> 00:07:34,883
At the stern,

195
00:07:34,883 --> 00:07:37,682
a set of control surfaces in a

196
00:07:37,682 --> 00:07:41,135
cross or X shape steers the submarine

197
00:07:41,135 --> 00:07:43,468
left, right, up and down.

198
00:07:43,648 --> 00:07:46,739
An X shape gives each surface a

199
00:07:46,739 --> 00:07:48,634
part of every move,

200
00:07:48,634 --> 00:07:52,124
and more control if one is damaged.

201
00:07:52,304 --> 00:07:55,255
Underwater flight is still flight,

202
00:07:55,255 --> 00:07:57,772
just in a much thicker fluid.

203
00:07:58,121 --> 00:08:01,418
Why are the propellers of modern submarines

204
00:08:01,418 --> 00:08:04,332
so secret, often covered when the boat

205
00:08:04,332 --> 00:08:05,482
is in dry dock?

206
00:08:05,662 --> 00:08:08,267
Because the shape of a propeller decides

207
00:08:08,267 --> 00:08:09,830
how much noise it makes.

208
00:08:10,010 --> 00:08:13,395
An enemy listening with sensitive microphones can

209
00:08:13,395 --> 00:08:15,881
identify a submarine by the sound of

210
00:08:15,881 --> 00:08:16,848
its propeller.

211
00:08:17,199 --> 00:08:20,733
Older submarines used a propeller with many

212
00:08:20,733 --> 00:08:24,185
curved blades, shaped to avoid cavitation,

213
00:08:24,185 --> 00:08:27,390
the bubbles that form and collapse when

214
00:08:27,390 --> 00:08:29,610
a propeller spins too fast.

215
00:08:29,790 --> 00:08:32,170
Those bubbles are loud underwater.

216
00:08:32,350 --> 00:08:35,659
Many modern submarines use a pump jet

217
00:08:35,659 --> 00:08:39,236
instead, a rotor spinning inside a duct,

218
00:08:39,236 --> 00:08:42,009
which is quieter at high speed.

219
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:44,670
That is why you may see tarps

220
00:08:44,670 --> 00:08:47,060
or panels hiding the back of a

221
00:08:47,060 --> 00:08:49,291
submarine in harbour photos.

222
00:08:49,471 --> 00:08:51,676
Its exact shape is one of the

223
00:08:51,676 --> 00:08:54,869
most guarded secrets in naval engineering.

224
00:08:55,049 --> 00:08:58,167
A submarine's whole value is in staying

225
00:08:58,167 --> 00:09:01,205
unheard, and the propeller is the part

226
00:09:01,205 --> 00:09:03,443
most likely to give it away.

227
00:09:03,794 --> 00:09:06,640
Why do some modern submarines have no

228
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:08,333
traditional periscope?

229
00:09:08,513 --> 00:09:11,346
A classic periscope is a long tube

230
00:09:11,346 --> 00:09:13,263
with mirrors or prisms,

231
00:09:13,263 --> 00:09:16,930
passing right through the pressure hull into

232
00:09:16,930 --> 00:09:18,347
the control room.

233
00:09:18,527 --> 00:09:20,860
That tube needs a large hole in

234
00:09:20,860 --> 00:09:23,418
the hull, and it decides where the

235
00:09:23,418 --> 00:09:24,998
control room must be,

236
00:09:24,998 --> 00:09:26,805
directly under the sail.

237
00:09:27,155 --> 00:09:31,304
The American Virginia class uses photonics masts

238
00:09:31,304 --> 00:09:31,996
instead.

239
00:09:32,176 --> 00:09:34,818
They carry cameras and sensors,

240
00:09:34,818 --> 00:09:37,802
and send images by cable to screens

241
00:09:37,802 --> 00:09:38,399
inside.

242
00:09:38,579 --> 00:09:41,225
Nothing has to pass through the hull

243
00:09:41,225 --> 00:09:43,944
except wires, so the control room can

244
00:09:43,944 --> 00:09:47,105
be placed lower and wherever designers want

245
00:09:47,105 --> 00:09:48,575
it, with more space.

246
00:09:48,925 --> 00:09:51,149
The mast can also take a quick

247
00:09:51,149 --> 00:09:53,967
panoramic picture in a moment and then

248
00:09:53,967 --> 00:09:56,265
go down, so it spends less time

249
00:09:56,265 --> 00:09:57,452
above the water,

250
00:09:57,452 --> 00:10:00,047
where it could be spotted by radar.

251
00:10:00,227 --> 00:10:02,630
The crew can study the image later.

252
00:10:02,810 --> 00:10:05,970
The periscope, the most famous part of

253
00:10:05,970 --> 00:10:08,881
a submarine, is being replaced by a

254
00:10:08,881 --> 00:10:09,463
camera.

255
00:10:09,813 --> 00:10:12,702
How can sailors escape from a sunken

256
00:10:12,702 --> 00:10:13,504
submarine?

257
00:10:13,684 --> 00:10:15,560
Through an escape trunk,

258
00:10:15,560 --> 00:10:17,827
a small chamber with a hatch,

259
00:10:17,827 --> 00:10:20,093
built into the pressure hull.

260
00:10:20,273 --> 00:10:21,563
A sailor enters,

261
00:10:21,563 --> 00:10:23,659
the inner hatch is closed,

262
00:10:23,659 --> 00:10:26,803
and the chamber is slowly flooded until

263
00:10:26,803 --> 00:10:30,351
the pressure inside matches the sea outside.

264
00:10:30,531 --> 00:10:32,435
Then the outer hatch can open.

265
00:10:32,785 --> 00:10:35,754
The sailor rises to the surface wearing

266
00:10:35,754 --> 00:10:36,897
a special suit,

267
00:10:36,897 --> 00:10:39,867
which provides air and keeps them warm.

268
00:10:40,047 --> 00:10:42,710
The rise must be fast but controlled,

269
00:10:42,710 --> 00:10:45,301
because the air in the lungs expands

270
00:10:45,301 --> 00:10:46,885
as the pressure drops.

271
00:10:47,065 --> 00:10:51,012
Escape is only possible from limited depths.

272
00:10:51,192 --> 00:10:54,598
Deeper, rescue vehicles must come down and

273
00:10:54,598 --> 00:10:56,301
connect to the hatch.

274
00:10:56,651 --> 00:10:59,967
Deep rescue submersibles can attach to the

275
00:10:59,967 --> 00:11:03,047
escape hatch like a docking spacecraft,

276
00:11:03,047 --> 00:11:05,337
and carry sailors up a few at

277
00:11:05,337 --> 00:11:07,390
a time in normal pressure.

278
00:11:07,570 --> 00:11:11,157
Navies around the world practise these rescues

279
00:11:11,157 --> 00:11:14,276
together, because when a submarine is in

280
00:11:14,276 --> 00:11:16,382
trouble, every hour counts.

281
00:11:16,732 --> 00:11:19,101
Why are the cabins of the deepest

282
00:11:19,101 --> 00:11:21,399
diving vehicles perfect spheres?

283
00:11:21,579 --> 00:11:24,614
Because a sphere is the strongest shape

284
00:11:24,614 --> 00:11:27,105
against pressure from all sides.

285
00:11:27,285 --> 00:11:28,728
In nineteen sixty,

286
00:11:28,728 --> 00:11:32,094
the bathyscaphe Trieste carried two men to

287
00:11:32,094 --> 00:11:34,819
the bottom of the Challenger Deep,

288
00:11:34,819 --> 00:11:37,223
almost eleven kilometres down.

289
00:11:37,403 --> 00:11:40,321
They sat inside a steel sphere about

290
00:11:40,321 --> 00:11:41,780
two metres across.

291
00:11:42,131 --> 00:11:43,197
At that depth,

292
00:11:43,197 --> 00:11:46,093
the pressure is more than one thousand

293
00:11:46,093 --> 00:11:48,075
times that at the surface.

294
00:11:48,255 --> 00:11:50,672
The walls of the Trieste's sphere were

295
00:11:50,672 --> 00:11:52,771
about thirteen centimetres thick.

296
00:11:52,951 --> 00:11:55,617
A long tube like a military submarine

297
00:11:55,617 --> 00:11:57,491
could never survive there.

298
00:11:57,671 --> 00:12:00,705
Only a sphere spreads the force evenly

299
00:12:00,705 --> 00:12:02,222
in every direction.

300
00:12:02,572 --> 00:12:04,733
The rest of the Trieste was a

301
00:12:04,733 --> 00:12:06,970
huge float filled with petrol,

302
00:12:06,970 --> 00:12:09,653
which is lighter than water and hard

303
00:12:09,653 --> 00:12:10,548
to compress.

304
00:12:10,728 --> 00:12:13,355
That is the same lesson as every

305
00:12:13,355 --> 00:12:14,176
submarine.

306
00:12:14,356 --> 00:12:15,798
The deeper you go,

307
00:12:15,798 --> 00:12:18,921
the simpler and stronger the shape must

308
00:12:18,921 --> 00:12:21,324
be, and the more you depend on

309
00:12:21,324 --> 00:12:22,926
the laws of physics,

310
00:12:22,926 --> 00:12:23,888
not on luck.

311
00:12:24,238 --> 00:12:25,677
So here are the ten.

312
00:12:25,857 --> 00:12:29,224
A round pressure hull with steel rings.

313
00:12:29,404 --> 00:12:31,355
A depth limit with a margin.

314
00:12:31,535 --> 00:12:34,402
Ballast tanks that blow out water.

315
00:12:34,582 --> 00:12:37,345
Trim tanks that keep perfect balance.

316
00:12:37,525 --> 00:12:40,021
Rubber tiles that swallow sound.

317
00:12:40,201 --> 00:12:42,941
Diving planes for underwater flight.

318
00:12:43,121 --> 00:12:45,594
Hidden propellers that stay quiet.

319
00:12:45,774 --> 00:12:48,189
Cameras instead of periscopes.

320
00:12:48,369 --> 00:12:50,807
Escape hatches for the worst day.

321
00:12:50,987 --> 00:12:53,274
And a perfect sphere for the deepest

322
00:12:53,274 --> 00:12:54,227
place on Earth.

323
00:12:54,577 --> 00:12:57,642
A submarine is a machine that fights

324
00:12:57,642 --> 00:12:59,004
the whole ocean,

325
00:12:59,004 --> 00:12:59,685
quietly.

326
00:12:59,865 --> 00:13:02,717
If you want more machines explained part

327
00:13:02,717 --> 00:13:05,426
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

328
00:13:05,606 --> 00:13:07,457
And tell us in the comments,

329
00:13:07,457 --> 00:13:09,243
would you dare to live on a

330
00:13:09,243 --> 00:13:11,028
submarine for three months?
